 Kubernetes Cluster Upgrade – Key Steps and Best Practices
Recently, I was revisiting Kubernetes cluster upgrade procedures and thought I'd share some important considerations that every DevOps Engineer should know.
✅ Why Upgrade Kubernetes?
Access to new features
Security patches and bug fixes
Better performance and stability
Support for latest APIs and tools
🔹 Pre-Upgrade Checklist
✔ Review Kubernetes Release Notes
kubectl version --short
✔ Verify Node Health
kubectl get nodes
kubectl top nodes
✔ Check Deprecated APIs
kubectl get apiservices
✔ Take ETCD Backup (for self-managed clusters)
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save backup.db
🔹 Upgrade Strategy
Upgrade Control Plane
Upgrade Worker Nodes
Drain Nodes Before Upgrade
kubectl drain <node-name> --ignore-daemonsets
Upgrade Node Components
Uncordon Node
kubectl uncordon <node-name>
🔹 Post-Upgrade Validation
✔ Verify all nodes are Ready
kubectl get nodes
✔ Check Pod Health
kubectl get pods -A
✔ Validate Critical Applications
✔ Monitor Logs and Metrics
Lessons Learned:
A successful Kubernetes upgrade is not just about running commands—it requires proper planning, backups, compatibility checks, and validation to avoid production downtime.
